 normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance among the groups should be approximately equal.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nTABLE 9.9 is applicable in situations where researchers need to explore the effects of multiple factors with varying levels. It is particularly useful when the research question involves understanding the interaction effects between two-level factors and their combined influence on a response variable represented by a three-level factor.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure following the use of TABLE 9.9 typically involves conducting an Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to assess the significance of the factors and their interactions. The results can be interpreted to determine which factors significantly affect the response variable and how they interact.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nThe strengths of using TABLE 9.9 include:\n- Enhanced understanding of complex interactions between factors.\n- Ability to systematically explore the effects of multiple levels on a response variable.\n- Facilitation of more efficient experimental designs by reducing the number of runs needed to explore multiple factors.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nLimitations of TABLE 9.9 include:\n- Complexity in interpretation, especially when dealing with multiple factors and levels.\n- Potential for increased experimental error if assumptions are violated.\n- The need for a larger sample size to adequately estimate the effects of the three-level factor.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated designs include factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, and response surface methodology. These methods also explore the effects of multiple factors but may differ in their approach to handling levels and interactions.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing the design represented in TABLE 9.9, researchers should consider the feasibility of conducting experiments